          </Overlay>
          <Pad Id="4bswLDLdcaWrspG4BBCXP4" Bounds="92,3160,506,198" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="The closing chapter. The synthesis. The reveal that Process, Record, and Class are not three separate things but one family. The reader meets the &quot;Enable as Process&quot; toggle, sees the small example of a Record running automatically when placed in the patch, and watches Dispose finally fire visibly. The chapter closes with a brief look back across Part V and a forward pointer to what comes after.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="7ExuzS2SENENhCvnpLpwFX" Bounds="92,3372,540,908" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="**1. The chapter does three things in sequence.** First it teaches the &quot;Enable as Process&quot; toggle and shows it on a small Counter. Second it uses the same mode to demonstrate Dispose finally firing visibly. Third it reveals the Process-Record-Class family and looks back at Part V. The three jobs reinforce each other - the Counter shows how the mode works, the Logger shows what Dispose finally does, and the family reveal makes both make sense in the context of everything that came before.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**2. The Counter and Logger are deliberately small.** Two of the dryest, most minimal examples in all of Part V. The chapter's job is synthesis, not demonstration of complex behavior. Small examples keep the focus on the conceptual unification.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**3. The &quot;two reasons for Dispose to fire&quot; column is honest but light.** This is the most technically nuanced moment in the chapter - Records and Classes have Dispose for slightly different underlying reasons. The column names this but does not dive deep. The reader who wants to understand resource cleanup more thoroughly has TobyK's tutorial and the Gray Book linked.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**4. The &quot;When to Reach For It&quot; column is direct.** One of these in your patch: enable Process. Many of these in a spread: don't. This is the practical rule the reader needs, named clearly.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**5. &quot;Looking Back at Part V&quot; does narrative work.** It is the only part of Part V that explicitly summarizes what came before. The chapter has earned the right to do this - it is the closer, and a brief look back is what a closer should provide.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**6. The forward pointer is gentle.** &quot;The next part of the tutorial - once you continue - picks up from here and looks at how your applications begin to interact with everything outside themselves.&quot; This previews the Emergent Mind section without naming it explicitly. The reader knows there is more coming if they want it; they are not promised a specific timeline.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**7. The cosmological theme is honored implicitly.** Part V is &quot;The Origin of Life.&quot; This chapter closes by saying the reader's program has become a thing that can run, persist, and end on its own - Create, Update, Dispose as a complete life arc. The metaphor lands without being labored.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**8. Length.** Roughly nine columns of body text plus two live patches (Counter and Logger). This is the longest chapter in Part V apart from chapter 41, which is appropriate - a closer should be substantial. Comparable in scope to chapter 11 (Process Nodes) and chapter 41 (Use Case II), the other two structural anchor points.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**9. What this chapter does *not* do.** It does not introduce new advanced topics like resource management, IDisposable in detail, or design patterns beyond a passing mention. It does not introduce new mutability concepts beyond what's been established. Its job is synthesis and closure, and it stays focused on that.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="2vNxG5bT5bbBqEeLyq2ltC" Bounds="661,3251,401,459" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="We have come a long way in Part V. We started with the recognition that Process nodes from chapter 11 were already objects - we just had not called them that. We then learned to define our own - Records first, then Classes - and built complete applications with both. We also learned when to choose each, and how to think about collections separately.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;In this final chapter we close the circle. Records and Classes can themselves become Process nodes, with a single setting in the Patch Explorer. When you flip it, your type can be placed directly in the patch, its Create runs on placement, its Update runs each frame, and its Dispose runs when removed. This was always available - we just did not use it, because the rest of Part V was about types as data, managed by hand.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;This is also where Dispose finally makes full sense. We mentioned it in chapter 41 as the third reserved-color operation. Here we will see it fire visibly, on both a Record and a Class - for slightly different reasons, which is the last small detail of the family.&#xD;&#xA;">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="72algSnMOG1PB2lGZbkjhU" Bounds="1129,3265,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="A Mode We Have Not Used">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="2YpjqSvIYuArGdeg7drSQY" Bounds="1129,3307,405,467" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Throughout Part V we have used Records and Classes as data. We created instances by calling Create, modified them by calling other operations, drew them by calling Draw, and stored them in pads or spreads to keep them around between frames. Every operation we used, we called by hand - placing nodes in the patch, wiring inputs, deciding when each one would run.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;There is another way to use a Record or Class. You can place the type itself directly into your patch, like a Process node. When you do, the type behaves as a Process - its Create operation runs once, the moment the node is placed. Its Update operation runs every frame, automatically. Its Dispose operation runs when the node is removed.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;This was available the whole time. We did not use it because Part V's use cases - bundling, dynamic instances, mutability comparisons - were about types as data being manipulated by hand. The Process behavior is the other mode of the same type. It is useful when you want one specific instance of a type to live in your patch and run by itself.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="1XiDvSgf92sSGjbUgGdb0E" Bounds="1578,3267,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Enabling Process Behavior">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="7j4pRJc58LLwrasOZvdNb2" Bounds="1578,3309,405,467" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="The setting is a single toggle in the Patch Explorer. Right-click your Record or Class definition, find &quot;Enable as Process&quot; in the menu, and turn it on. Nothing about the type changes - its properties stay, its operations stay, the existing places in your patch where you create instances by hand keep working.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;What changes is that you can now also place the type itself as a node, by typing its name in the Node Browser. The node has no input pin for State Input - it does not need one, it is the instance. Its Create runs when the node is placed. Its Update runs on every frame the patch is running. Its Dispose runs when the node is removed from the patch, when the patch closes, or when the application stops.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The reader who has worked through Part V can now recognize that this is exactly how Process nodes from chapter 11 have always worked. The &quot;Enable as Process&quot; toggle is the bridge that reveals it.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="4y5yWhs0wPOVhOcNdwzpbE" Bounds="2048,3273,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="The Counter Example">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="71hN8GtcSgHNfKyeME0Q9u" Bounds="2048,3315,405,339" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="In the patch on the right we have defined a small Record called Counter, with a single property - Count, an integer. It has an Update operation that increments Count by one each frame. With &quot;Enable as Process&quot; turned on, we place the Counter directly in the patch.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The Counter starts at zero on placement. Every frame, Update fires, incrementing Count. An IOBox connected to the Counter shows the count climbing. There is no spread, no pad, no manual call to Update - the type is running by itself.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;This is a singleton pattern at its simplest. One Counter, placed once, running for as long as the patch is open. If you want it to do more, you add more operations. If you want it to reset on a key press, you wire that in. The Counter is a Process node in everything but origin - it just happens to also be a Record, which means it could also be used as data elsewhere if you wanted.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="5PFBky8rv9BBRwLVicJqGc" Bounds="2048,3655,401,228" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="**Live element (x=1092, y=820):**&#xD;&#xA;- A `Counter` Record (Process enabled) placed directly in the patch.&#xD;&#xA;- An IOBox showing the current Count value climbing.&#xD;&#xA;- A second IOBox showing &quot;frames since placement&quot; to make the per-frame Update behavior visible.&#xD;&#xA;">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">7</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="5ihi9s8Qxz2DNW9ktqXGlP" Bounds="866,3864,277,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="ORDER OF OPERATIONS!">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="16NJYniCJZUaUlQV6XT43Q" Bounds="2517,3285,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Dispose, Properly Demonstrated">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="68HVg1EhzAEaswMtiqBftP" Bounds="2517,3327,404,369" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="We mentioned Dispose in chapter 41 as the third reserved-color operation - the dark-red one - that runs when an instance is destroyed. Until now we did not have a clear, demonstrable moment for it to fire. With Process-enabled types, we do.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The patch on the right shows a Record called Logger, with a Create operation that prints &quot;Logger started&quot; to the console, an Update operation that does nothing visible, and a Dispose operation that prints &quot;Logger stopped.&quot; With Process enabled, we place the Logger in the patch and watch its messages appear.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;When the patch loads, Create fires and the console reads &quot;Logger started.&quot; Each frame, Update fires (silently, because we patched nothing into it). When you delete the Logger node from the patch - or when you close the patch entirely - Dispose fires, and the console reads &quot;Logger stopped.&quot;&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;This is Dispose in its purest form. A clear moment for it to fire, a clear behavior to observe.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="7ebr4p2yzSqWTUjgKqH3Eb" Bounds="2517,3716,401,228" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="**Live element (x=92, y=1300):**&#xD;&#xA;- A `Logger` Record with Process enabled, with Create, Update, and Dispose operations defined.&#xD;&#xA;- Create logs &quot;Logger started&quot; via a Log or Print node.&#xD;&#xA;- Dispose logs &quot;Logger stopped.&quot;&#xD;&#xA;- An IOBox or console region showing the log output.&#xD;&#xA;- Instructions in an annotation IOBox: &quot;Delete the Logger node and watch the console.&quot;&#xD;&#xA;">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">7</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="1fBPKLDxPmCZIQYiWZUmd0" Bounds="2989,3281,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Two Reasons for Dispose to Fire">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="6WQv5Och0jJRZuYcvkwwvc" Bounds="2989,3323,404,399" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="A small detail worth knowing. Dispose fires for both Records and Classes when they are used as Process nodes - but for subtly different reasons.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;For a Class with Process enabled, Dispose fires when the instance is destroyed. The Class has identity. The runtime knows when the last reference to it goes away, and at that moment Dispose runs. This is the standard reference-based story you would expect from any language with reference types.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;For a Record with Process enabled, Dispose fires when the specific placement is removed. The Record itself, being a value, does not really get destroyed in the same way - but the runtime tracks the placement of the node, and when the placement goes away, the Record running there has its Dispose called.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The end result is the same - you can put logic in Dispose that runs when the type is no longer in the patch - but the underlying mechanism differs slightly. It is a tiny detail that becomes important only when you start wrapping resources that need careful cleanup, which is outside this tutorial's scope.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="0hBq7wIck8KLFKWK3jiUbl" Bounds="3463,3282,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="When to Reach For It">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="44RfoP2y7Yz9o50oP2V3R3" Bounds="3463,3324,404,433" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="&quot;Enable as Process&quot; is for the place-and-forget case. When you want one specific instance of a type to exist in your patch and run autonomously, this is the mode for it.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Singletons. A global audio engine that initializes once and runs for the life of the application. A debug overlay that draws each frame. A configuration manager that loads settings on Create and saves them on Dispose. These are all single, persistent instances - not collections that grow and shrink.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;When you do not want this mode is when you have many instances created and destroyed at runtime - the patterns from chapter 41. There, you want types used as data, with spreads and pads doing the management. Mixing the two - a Process-enabled type used in a spread - generally does not work the way you would expect, because the Process behavior assumes one specific placement, not many.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The decision is straightforward: one of these in your patch, or many of these in a spread? If one, Enable as Process. If many, leave it off and use the patterns from earlier in Part V.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="3ePgpf0mIUPKiRtvS9C3sd" Bounds="3936,3283,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="The Family Revealed">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="5GPD6xjPaYcV2mpggVtrqV" Bounds="3936,3325,404,433" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Looking at all of this together, Process, Record, and Class come into focus as a single family of choices about how to define and use objects.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;A Process node from chapter 11 is essentially a type with Process behavior enabled by default and no support for runtime instances. It is placed at edit time, runs by itself, and that is all.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;A Record is the default object type. You can use it as data - passed through operations as values - or you can enable Process behavior and place it directly in your patch as a self-running unit. Both modes work; the type itself does not change.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;A Class is the mutable counterpart. Same flexibility - usable as data via reference, or with Process behavior enabled - but with reference semantics and in-place modification.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The three names that seemed like three different things at the start of Part V are different settings on the same underlying machinery. The unity was always there. The chapter just made it visible.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="5TBqgjvr83tLcKy0jxEY2H" Bounds="4421,3282,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Looking Back at Part V">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="6FNDr6dBLwcZSp3hR1b0QL" Bounds="4421,3324,404,433" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Part V started with the recognition that you had been using objects since chapter 10 and defining them since chapter 11. It ends with the recognition that Process, Record, and Class are one family.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;In between you learned to define your own data types from the ground up. You gave them properties and custom operations. You used them to bundle related data into clean spreads. You created and destroyed instances at runtime in response to user input. You met the mutability fork and learned to choose between Records and Classes deliberately. You met the collection question and learned to separate it from the object question.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;That is a lot. If you absorbed even half of it, you have moved from being a patcher who uses what vvvv provides to being one who extends vvvv with your own building blocks. The next part of the tutorial - once you continue - picks up from here and looks at how your applications begin to interact with everything outside themselves.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Overlay Id="5zJX7zBBaZwkO3RZ32ZPuI" Bounds="5032,3347,383,298">
            <p:ColorIndex p:Type="Int32">10</p:ColorIndex>
          </Overlay>
